# Level00

## Reconstructed source

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void)
{
    int password;

    puts("***********************************");
    puts("*            -Level00 -           *");
    puts("***********************************");
    printf("Password:");
    scanf("%d", &password);
    if (password == 5276)
    {
        puts("Authenticated!");
        system("/bin/sh");
        return (0);
    }
    puts("Invalid Password!");
    return (1);
}
```

## Vulnerability

The password is hardcoded in the binary. `main` reads a single integer with
`scanf("%d", &password)` and compares it to the constant `5276` before granting
a shell. Decompiling the binary exposes that constant directly.

The binary is setuid `level01`, so the shell spawned by `system("/bin/sh")`
runs with level01 privileges. Recovering the secret only requires reading the
binary; nothing in memory has to be corrupted.

## Exploitation

```sh
./level00
# Password: 5276
# Authenticated!
whoami                           # level01
cat /home/users/level01/.pass
```

